AB  - The inefficient management of medical records in physical format in Honduras public and private healthcare facilities represents a significant problem in medical care. The manual search for records, prolonged by the need to locate them by ID number, consumes valuable medical staff time and exposes the records to environmental risks, such as flooding, causing possible loss of critical information. The overall objective of the project was to develop a medical informatics system that would allow appointment management and the recording of preclinical and ophthalmologic care data at the private clinic in Tegucigalpa. The method used included a literature review, detailed interviews with clinical staff, and a comparative evaluation of different EHR systems. OpenEMR was then implemented and adapted to the specific workflows of the clinic, followed by staff training and feedback collection for continuous improvements. The best result obtained was the implementation of the medical information management software, achieving workflow optimization, reducing the process by 30 percent and eliminating the use of paper formats. In conclusion, a medical informatics system was developed, which includes modules for appointment management, pre-clinic data registration, and also the inclusion of an ophthalmology section.
