AB  - Digital health services can be categorized as the way in which digital and mobile technologies are being used to support health system needs. Across the globe, during the pandemic (COVID-19) countries suspended all their health services including outpatient departments also. This study aims to understand and bridge the research gap w.r.t to Mobile Health Applications by recommending and analytically confirming a theoretical model which can capture vital aspects of the user's perception. The researchers used UTUAT to predict the behaviour of user's acceptance of digital health services. An empirical study has been conducted in India using the survey method. Following the analysis, the researchers observed that, Performance expectancy has been found to be the most prominent factor prophesying e-satisfaction and persistent intent. This establishes the significance of cognitive and functional benefits of Mobile Health Applications (MHAs) from customers' perspective. Data was then probed by utilizing and applying the structural equation modelling (SEM). The outcomes mostly reinforced and validated the current study's model. A rich result was presented considering the nature of MHAs and customers. Based on these results, there is adequate support for the application developers and decision-makers in India to build appropriate infrastructure for Mobile health applications.
