AB  - Due to the ageing population healthcare systems face monumental economic and social challenges. To respond to them, e-Health technologies and services are developed that seek to improve the quality of care and to support the monitoring and management of chronic diseases. One decisive factor for the widespread use of these technologies is user acceptance. As many e-Health technologies are essentially based on the storage, analysis, and transmission of large amounts of health-related data, privacy concerns can be a serious impediment to the successful adoption. However, privacy concerns are not included in traditional technology acceptance models. In this study, the impact of privacy concerns on the acceptance of e-Health technologies is studied. Also benefits and the formation of privacy concerns through trust in data protection, perceived data sensitivity, and the individual disposition to value privacy are examined. N = 253 participants with chronic health conditions evaluated two exemplary e- Health technologies: fitness trackers and remote monitoring of implanted cardiac devices. Structural equation modelling shows that privacy concerns are indeed an important barrier to the adoption of e-Health technologies and are influenced by the perceived data sensitivity, trust in data protection, and the individual disposition to value privacy. Users' privacy perceptions and concerns should be taken into account when designing e-Health technologies.
