AB  - Mobile Health (M-Health) technology enhances healthcare accessibility and efficiency but faces challenges due to the lack of a real-time surveillance system for emergency responses. This study investigates the factors influencing M-Health acceptance among Indonesian citizens, specifically focusing on Behavioral Intention, Trust, Facilitating Conditions, Performance Expectancy, Social Influence, Effort Expectancy, Habit, Satisfaction, Usage Intention, and Usage Behavior. Data were collected from 216 respondents in Indonesia through purposive sampling in June 2024. Quantitative analysis was employed to evaluate the relationships between these variables. The findings reveal that Behavioral Intention, Effort Expectancy, and Social Influence do not significantly affect Satisfaction, while other factors show varying degrees of influence on M-Health acceptance. Understanding these dynamics is essential for developing effective strategies to enhance M-Health adoption and efficiency in Indonesia, ultimately improving healthcare outcomes and user satisfaction with M-Health services.
