AB  - The COVID-19 pandemic has exposed significant flaws in the exchange of electronic health information (EHI) in the United States. EHI interoperability is a top priority for the Center for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health IT (ONC). In July 2021 these agencies began enforcing legal mandates under the 21st Century Cures Act with the goal of achieving major EHI interoperability throughout the US by 2025. If this goal can be met, then EHI interoperability has the potential to trigger major disruption in the healthcare industry and realignment during the coming decade. Toward this end, CMS and ONC have embraced the Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources (FHIR) framework as promoted by the Health Level Seven International (HL7) standards organization. A key unanswered question is whether HL7/FHIR is mature enough to support this national goal. This paper assesses the HL7/FHIR technology framework and the FHIR Accelerator known as the Da Vinci Project to forecast its adoption potential. Historical forecasting analogies as well as the PRISM methodology are used. The analysis identifies likely adoption risks, forecasts expected patterns of adopter behavior, and recommends strategies for mitigate risks.
