AB  - A divergence-preserving hybrid implicit–explicit finite-difference time-domain (DP-HIE-FDTD) method is proposed for accurate and efficient electromagnetic (EM) simulations. In contrast to traditional semi-implicit or fully implicit finite-difference time-domain (FDTD) schemes, which often fail to preserve the divergence condition required by Gauss’s law—leading to potential inaccuracies—the proposed method provides a more physically faithful solution. It strictly enforces the divergence-free condition in source-free regions, effectively eliminating nonphysical artifacts. To simulate open-region problems, a complex frequency-shifted perfectly matched layer (CFS-PML) is incorporated into the DP-HIE-FDTD framework, enabling stable and accurate truncation of computational domains. Subsequently, a comprehensive analysis is carried out to evaluate the method’s DP property, numerical stability, dispersion characteristics, and computational complexity. The accuracy and effectiveness of the DP-HIE-FDTD method are finally validated through several representative numerical examples, including a frequency-selective surface (FSS), a slot-loaded shielding structure, a microstrip branch-line coupler, and a dual-band microstrip patch antenna.
